{
  "per_column": [
    {
      "name": "primary_issue_category",
      "analytical_usefulness": 0.95,
      "source_groundedness": 0.85,
      "hallucination_observed": true,
      "rationale": "Directly identifies ticket types, the core grouping axis for 'which types create heaviest workload' and resolution-time analysis. Categories are soundly derived from short_description in most samples, but the 'Unable to access the company's intranet' row was labeled database_access, which is a mis-categorization (intranet != database), indicating some ungrounded assignment."
    },
    {
      "name": "assigned_agent",
      "analytical_usefulness": 0.85,
      "source_groundedness": 0.4,
      "hallucination_observed": true,
      "rationale": "Agent identity is essential for measuring per-agent workload. However the original table already has assigned_to/closed_by, so this may be a restatement; more importantly, the source_text samples provided do not expose any agent-name field, so the specific agent values (e.g. beth_anglin, howard_johnson) are not visibly derivable from the shown evidence and appear asserted. If they map from an existing column they'd be grounded, but the evidence given does not support them."
    },
    {
      "name": "issue_severity_signal",
      "analytical_usefulness": 0.25,
      "source_groundedness": 0.5,
      "hallucination_observed": true,
      "rationale": "A bare true/false severity flag adds little leverage for the workload/resolution-time query, especially since priority already exists in the original table. All samples are 'False' with no visible textual basis for the true/false split, so the values are weakly grounded and the signal is near-constant and generic."
    }
  ],
  "analytical_usefulness": 0.72,
  "source_groundedness": 0.58,
  "rationale": "primary_issue_category is the key column enabling the requested 'which ticket types drive workload / resolution time' analysis and is largely well-grounded in short_description, though one mislabel is visible. assigned_agent is useful for workload attribution but overlaps with existing assignment columns and its specific values are not supported by the shown evidence. issue_severity_signal is a generic near-constant flag redundant with priority and lacks clear textual grounding. Overall the set provides meaningful grouping axes but with notable groundedness concerns on two of three columns."
}

